I want you to write something similar to
moonlib.mapops.MapOperations.GenerateLightingFunction() that uses the
GPU using ILGPU.

You can use that code for ideas as well as moonlib.horizon.QuadTreeHorizonGenerator.GenerateHorizonsForPatches().

In this step, we'll solve part of the problem.

This code will be provided with:
1. dem_path: The path for an moonlib.horzin.ElevationMap (a Digital Elevation Map)
2. times: A list of DateTimes

In C# code, the program needs to:
1. Load the DEM
1a. Confirm that the DEM's width is a multiple of 128 and its height is a multiple of 128
2. Generate a list of vectors toward the sun from the moon in MOON_ME frame from the list of DateTimes
3. Generate a list of vectors toward the earth from the moon in MOON_ME frame from the list of DateTimes
4. Convert each list of Vector3d into a list of Vector3 (from double to single precision)

5. Allocate a GPU accelerator
6. Copy the ElevationMap to the GPU
7. Copy both lists of vectors to the GPU

We're going to operate on 128 x 128 pixel patches of the DEM.
In a later version of this, we will also load horizon files for each patch that we process.

The result will be an array of floats representing the elevation of the sun in the coordinate system of each pixel in the patch.

8. Allocate space for the result.

This should probably be a 3D array of size 128 x 128 x time.Count.  I'm not sure which order of indices will be the most efficient for coelescing writes.

Launch a kernel that operates on the elevation map, the lists of vectors and writes the output array.

This kernel shall be parallel over the pixels of the patch.
For each pixel:
1. Generate an array corresponding to the Matrix4d generated by the existing ElevationMap.GetMoonMEToENU() function
2. Convert this from an array of double precision values to an array of single precision values.
Note: This must be done after fully constructing the array in double precision, because single precision match isn't accurate enough for some of the small angles and subtraction of large Float64 values.

For each sun vector, generate the azimuth and elevation angles following what ElevationMap.GetAzEl does.

Ignore the azimuth angle for now.
Convert the elevation angle from radians to degrees and write it into the output array at
the proper place.

When this kernel finishes, copy the result array back to the CPU.

Process all of the patches in the DEM in a stream.
Consider using multiple streams to allow parallelism of 2 to 4 to keep the GPU filled.

If you have any questions, ask them before starting.

Write this code into a class called moonlib.horizon.Lightmaps and a method called GenerateElevationArray().
